### Auth module — manual requests (REST Client extension for VS Code)
#
# How to use:
# 1. Install the "REST Client" extension (humao.rest-client) in VS Code.
# 2. Open this file. Above each request block you'll see a small
#    "Send Request" link — click it to fire that specific request.
# 3. Make sure the API is running (npm run dev) before sending requests.
# 4. Edit the values in the JSON bodies freely to try different cases.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000

### Register a new user (happy path)
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Ada Lovelace",
  "email": "ada.lovelace@example.com",
  "password": "supersecret123"
}

### Login with the user created above
# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "ada.lovelace@example.com",
  "password": "supersecret123"
}

# The request above is named "login" (see the "# @name login" comment).
# REST Client lets you reuse its response in later requests, e.g. once
# protected endpoints exist (bookings, rooms), you'd add a header like:
#
#   Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.$.accessToken}}
#
# There's nothing to attach it to yet since /rooms and /bookings aren't
# implemented, but keep this pattern in mind for when they are.

### Error case: register with an email that's already taken
# Same email as the successful register above — expect 409 Conflict.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Ada Lovelace (duplicate)",
  "email": "ada.lovelace@example.com",
  "password": "anotherpassword123"
}

### Error case: login with the wrong password
# Correct email, wrong password — expect 401 Unauthorized.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "ada.lovelace@example.com",
  "password": "wrongpassword"
}
